 {
  "response": "I've created `/app/filter.py`. It edits the file in place and only rewrites it when something dangerous is found. It works directly on the raw text rather than re-serialising parsed HTML, so everything it keeps stays byte-for-byte the same.\n\n**Testing:** a sample clean page (doctype, CSS, tables, headings, entities, comments, forms, a `data:image/png` image) came out byte-for-byte unchanged. Two files with 42 XSS vectors in total triggered 14 alerts in headless Chromium inside the container before filtering, and none after.\n\n**What it removes:**\n- **Script elements:** `<script>` together with its content, including nested tricks like `<scr<script>…ipt>` and a script left unclosed at the end of the file. It keeps re-filtering until nothing changes, so pieces that join up into a new script tag also get removed.\n- **Embedding tags:** the tags for `iframe`, `frame`, `frameset`, `object`, `embed`, `applet`, `base` and `portal` are removed, but the text between them is kept.\n- **Dangerous attributes:** only the attribute itself is cut out; the rest of the tag is left alone.\n  - Every `on*` event handler, plus `srcdoc`.\n  - Any value containing `javascript:` or `vbscript:`. This is checked after decoding entities, CSS escapes and hidden whitespace/control characters, so `jav&#x09;ascript:` and `&#106;…&colon;` are caught.\n  - `data:` URLs, unless they are plain raster images (png, jpeg, gif, webp and similar).\n  - SVG `attributeName` values that target `href`, `src` or event handlers.\n  - `meta` refresh redirects to `javascript:` or `data:` URLs.\n- **Unsafe CSS:** inline styles and `<style>` contents containing `expression(`, `javascript:`, `behavior:`, `-moz-binding` or `@import`. For a `<style>` block, the whole content is emptied.\n- **Tag-boundary tricks:** each page is scanned two ways, once respecting quotes and once breaking at the first `>`. That catches both `<a title=\">\" onclick=…>` and break-outs like `<noscript><p title=\"</noscript><img onerror=…>\">` (the same works for `textarea`, `title` and comments).\n\n**Where it removes more than strictly needed:**\n- An attribute is dropped if its text merely contains `javascript:`, even in a harmless place like a `title`.\n- A self-closing `<script/>` inside SVG is treated like a normal script tag. Everything from it up to the next `</script>` is removed, which can take legitimate content with it.\n\nI deleted my test files afterwards, so `filter.py` is the only file in `/app`.",
